  Since \\(n-k\\le 3n-3k\\) for all \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), we can always keep a suitable set of \\(n-k\\) lines.  \n\n3. **Construction for a given \\(k\\).**  \n   - **\\(k=0\\).**  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   - **\\(1\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n     1. Choose the points  \n\n        \\[\n        P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n        \\]\n\n        They belong to \\(T_n\\) and are pairwise distinct.  \n     2. For each \\(i\\) pick a real number \\(\\alpha_i\\) with \\(\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\) for \\(i\\neq j\\).  Define the sunny line  \n\n        \\[\n        L_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n        \\]\n\n        Then \\(L_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\) and, because its slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), it contains no other point of \\(T_n\\).  Hence the \\(L_i\\) are distinct and sunny.  \n     3. Remove the set \\(\\{P_i\\}_{i=1}^k\\) from \\(T_n\\).  The remaining points are still covered by the three families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\).  \n     4. From \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) discard any \\(3k\\) lines that intersect the removed points (at most three lines per removed point).  The set that remains still covers every point of \\(T_n\\setminus\\{P_i\\}\\).  Because the original union contains \\(3n\\) lines, after discarding at most \\(3k\\) we still have at least \\(3n-3k\\) lines.  Since \\(n-k\\le 3n-3k\\) for all admissible \\(k\\), we can select a sub‑collection of exactly \\(n-k\\) lines from the remaining ones; denote them by \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}\\).  \n   - The family  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k,\\;\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}\\}\n     \\]\n\n     consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line (the points \\(P_i\\) are on the sunny lines \\(L_i\\); all other points are on one of the \\(\\ell_j\\)).  \n\n4. **Verification.**  \n   - Distinctness: the sunny lines have pairwise distinct slopes, none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the non‑sunny lines belong to \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) and have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) respectively, so they cannot coincide with any sunny line.  Within each family the chosen lines have different intercepts, hence are distinct.  \n   - Coverage: by construction each point of \\(T_n\\) is either a special point (covered by its sunny line) or belongs to the set covered by the selected non‑sunny lines.  \n   - Count: we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, totalling \\(n\\) lines.  \n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Existence of a configuration with any prescribed number of sunny lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at\nleast one of the lines.\n\n*Proof.*\n\n**Case \\(k=0\\).**  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAll have slope \\(\\infty\\) (hence are non‑sunny) and they clearly contain every\npoint \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) because the first coordinate of any such point satisfies\n\\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  Thus a configuration with no sunny line exists.\n\n**Case \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n1. **Choice of special points.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i).\n   \\]\n\n 