real numbers \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n   \\]\n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\).  Because its slope is different from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), the only point of \\(T_n\\) that can lie on \\(L_i\\) is\n   \\(P_i\\) itself (any two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) have slope \\(0\\),\n   \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  Consequently the lines \\(L_i\\) are pairwise\n   distinct and sunny.\n\n3. **Removal of the special points.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T'_n = T_n\\setminus\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(T'_n\\) lies on at least one line of the three families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, for any \\((a,b)\\in T'_n\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), the vertical\n   line \\(x=a\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\) all belong to (1) and contain\n   \\((a,b)\\).\n\n4. **Selection of \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.**  \n   The union \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) consists of exactly\n   \\(3n\\) distinct lines.  Each removed point \\(P_i\\) belongs to precisely three\n   lines of this union (its horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines).  Hence\n   at most \\(3k\\) lines of the union become *essential* for covering the set\n   \\(T'_n\\); the other lines are still present and can be used freely.\n\n   Because \\(3n-3k\\ge n-k\\) for all \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), after discarding the at\n   most \\(3k\\) essential lines we still have at least \\(n-k\\) lines left.\n   Choose any \\(n-k\\) of the remaining lines; denote them by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}.\n   \\]\n\n   By construction each point of \\(T'_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen\n   \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\n5. **The final family.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k,\\;\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\))\n   and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the \\(\\ell_j\\)), altogether \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered: the points \\(P_i\\) lie on the\n   sunny lines \\(L_i\\); any other point belongs to \\(T'_n\\) and is covered by\n   one of the \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\nThus a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for\nwhich there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the\nstatement is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 1 shows that for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration\nexists.  Conversely, any configuration consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, so\nthe number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.  Hence\nthe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers between \\(0\\) and\n\\(n\\), inclusive. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
