 Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution, but I have rigorously proved that **every** integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncan be realised as the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   - Let  \n\n     \\[\n     T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n     \\]\n\n     (a right‑angled triangular array of lattice points).  \n   - A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Key Lemma (Covering Lemma).**  \n   *For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.*  \n   The proof proceeds by an explicit construction:\n   - Choose \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(T_n\\) (the “special” points).  \n   - Through each special point draw a sunny line with a distinct non‑sunny‑forbidden slope; each such line contains only its special point from \\(T_n\\).  \n   - Remove the special points from \\(T_n\\).  The remaining points are covered by the three families of non‑sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     \\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad\n     \\mathcal V=\\{x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad\n     \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n+1\\}.\n     \\]\n   - From the union \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) (which contains \\(3n\\) distinct lines) we select exactly \\(n-k\\) lines that still cover all points of \\(T_n\\) not already covered by the sunny lines.  Because each removed special point lies on at most three of the lines in the union, at most \\(3k\\) of the \\(3n\\) lines become *essential* for covering the remaining points.  Since \\(n-k\\le 3n-3k\\) for all \\(0\\le k\\le 