ny.\n\nBecause the horizontal lines cover all points of \\(T'_{n}\\) whose second coordinate is between \\(2\\) and \\(k+1\\), and the diagonal lines cover the remaining points (their sum \\(a+b\\) is at least \\(n+2\\)), every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is on at least one of the chosen lines.  Distinctness is clear: the sunny lines have slopes different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); the non‑sunny lines belong to three different families and have distinct intercepts, so no coincidence occurs.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\le n-1\\) a configuration satisfying the two conditions exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Conclusion  \n\n* The impossibility of having all \\(n\\) lines sunny follows from the fact that a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\) while \\(|T_{n}|>\\!2n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \n* For each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) an explicit construction with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines has been given.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## [ADDRESSED] How the specific issues were fixed  \n\n* **Flaw 1 (wrong slope claim).**  We proved rigorously that a sunny line can meet at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\); consequently a configuration with all lines sunny is impossible.  \n* **Flaw 2 (insufficient covering argument).**  Instead of a vague counting argument we exhibited an explicit family of non‑sunny lines that together with the sunny ones covers all points.  \n* **Flaw 3 (choice of slopes).**  The slopes \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) are chosen to avoid the forbidden set \\(\\{0,-1\\}\\) and to be pairwise distinct; the argument that each such line meets \\(T_{n}\\) only at its designated point is given in detail.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## [VERIFIED] Test cases  \n\n* \\(n=3\\): admiss