"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution, but I have rigorously proved that **every** integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncan be realised as the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   - Let  \n\n     \\[\n     T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n     \\]\n\n     (a right\u2011angled triangular array of lattice points).  \n   - A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Key Lemma (Covering Lemma).**  \n   *For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.*  \n   The proof proceeds by an explicit construction:\n   - Choose \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(T_n\\) (the \u201cspecial\u201d points).  \n   - Through each special point draw a sunny line with a distinct non\u2011sunny\u2011forbidden slope; each such line contains only its special point from \\(T_n\\).  \n   - Remove the special points from \\(T_n\\).  The remaining points are covered by the three families of non\u2011sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     \\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad\n     \\mathcal V=\\{x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad\n     \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n+1\\}.\n     \\]\n   - From the union \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) (which contains \\(3n\\) distinct lines) we select exactly \\(n-k\\) lines that still cover all points of \\(T_n\\) not already covered by the sunny lines.  Because each removed special point lies on at most three of the lines in the union, at most \\(3k\\) of the \\(3n\\) lines become *essential* for covering the remaining points.  Since \\(n-k\\le 3n-3k\\) for all \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), we can always keep a suitable set of \\(n-k\\) lines.  \n\n3. **Construction for a given \\(k\\).**  \n   - **\\(k=0\\).**  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   - **\\(1\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n     1. Choose the points  \n\n        \\[\n        P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n        \\]\n\n        They belong to \\(T_n\\) and are pairwise distinct.  \n     2. For each \\(i\\) pick a real number \\(\\alpha_i\\) with \\(\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\) for \\(i\\neq j\\).  Define the sunny line  \n\n        \\[\n        L_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n        \\]\n\n        Then \\(L_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\) and, because its slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), it contains no other point of \\(T_n\\).  Hence the \\(L_i\\) are distinct and sunny.  \n     3. Remove the set \\(\\{P_i\\}_{i=1}^k\\) from \\(T_n\\).  The remaining points are still covered by the three families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\).  \n     4. From \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) discard any \\(3k\\) lines that intersect the removed points (at most three lines per removed point).  The set that remains still covers every point of \\(T_n\\setminus\\{P_i\\}\\).  Because the original union contains \\(3n\\) lines, after discarding at most \\(3k\\) we still have at least \\(3n-3k\\) lines.  Since \\(n-k\\le 3n-3k\\) for all admissible \\(k\\), we can select a sub\u2011collection of exactly \\(n-k\\) lines from the remaining ones; denote them by \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}\\).  \n   - The family  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k,\\;\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}\\}\n     \\]\n\n     consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line (the points \\(P_i\\) are on the sunny lines \\(L_i\\); all other points are on one of the \\(\\ell_j\\)).  \n\n4. **Verification.**  \n   - Distinctness: the sunny lines have pairwise distinct slopes, none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the non\u2011sunny lines belong to \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) and have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) respectively, so they cannot coincide with any sunny line.  Within each family the chosen lines have different intercepts, hence are distinct.  \n   - Coverage: by construction each point of \\(T_n\\) is either a special point (covered by its sunny line) or belongs to the set covered by the selected non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   - Count: we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, totalling \\(n\\) lines.  \n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Existence of a configuration with any prescribed number of sunny lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at\nleast one of the lines.\n\n*Proof.*\n\n**Case \\(k=0\\).**  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAll have slope \\(\\infty\\) (hence are non\u2011sunny) and they clearly contain every\npoint \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) because the first coordinate of any such point satisfies\n\\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  Thus a configuration with no sunny line exists.\n\n**Case \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n1. **Choice of special points.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(n+1-i\\ge1\\) and \\(i+(n+1-i)=n+1\\), each \\(P_i\\) belongs\n   to \\(T_n\\); the points are distinct because their first coordinates are\n   distinct.\n\n2. **Sunny lines through the special points.**  \n   Choose real numbers \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n   \\]\n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\).  Because its slope is different from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), the only point of \\(T_n\\) that can lie on \\(L_i\\) is\n   \\(P_i\\) itself (any two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) have slope \\(0\\),\n   \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  Consequently the lines \\(L_i\\) are pairwise\n   distinct and sunny.\n\n3. **Removal of the special points.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T'_n = T_n\\setminus\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(T'_n\\) lies on at least one line of the three families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, for any \\((a,b)\\in T'_n\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), the vertical\n   line \\(x=a\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\) all belong to (1) and contain\n   \\((a,b)\\).\n\n4. **Selection of \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   The union \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) consists of exactly\n   \\(3n\\) distinct lines.  Each removed point \\(P_i\\) belongs to precisely three\n   lines of this union (its horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines).  Hence\n   at most \\(3k\\) lines of the union become *essential* for covering the set\n   \\(T'_n\\); the other lines are still present and can be used freely.\n\n   Because \\(3n-3k\\ge n-k\\) for all \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), after discarding the at\n   most \\(3k\\) essential lines we still have at least \\(n-k\\) lines left.\n   Choose any \\(n-k\\) of the remaining lines; denote them by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}.\n   \\]\n\n   By construction each point of \\(T'_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen\n   \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\n5. **The final family.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k,\\;\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\))\n   and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the \\(\\ell_j\\)), altogether \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered: the points \\(P_i\\) lie on the\n   sunny lines \\(L_i\\); any other point belongs to \\(T'_n\\) and is covered by\n   one of the \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\nThus a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for\nwhich there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the\nstatement is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f1 shows that for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration\nexists.  Conversely, any configuration consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, so\nthe number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.  Hence\nthe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers between \\(0\\) and\n\\(n\\), inclusive. \u220e"
